  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/badis-autumnum-2/",
      "html": "<p>Badis autumnum was formally described only in 2015 and is called the \"red spotted badis\" in the international trade. Maximum size is about 6 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/badis-autumnum-2/",
      "html": "<p>Badis autumnum originates from northern India and needs no additional heater for most of the year. Only for breeding should the temperature be raised to 24–26°C; most of the year 18–22°C is enough, and in winter temperatures can be as low as 14–16°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/badis-autumnum-2/",
      "html": "<p>All species of chameleon fish avoid dry food, but frozen food is readily accepted, as is live food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/badis-autumnum-2/",
      "html": "<p>The number of species of chameleon fish has increased drastically in the past years. Until 2002 only one genus and one species with four subspecies were accepted; now there are two genera (Badis and Dario) with 23 species in Badis and 6 in Dario. Like all members of the genus Badis, the fish can change colour very fast, hence the name chameleon fish.</p><p>Three components of the pattern allow the species to be recognised despite all colour changes: the broad band behind the eyes, combined with the lack of a so-called cleithral spot on the shoulder (present in most other species), and the tail spot that resembles a T lying on its side. In most moods the fish also shows a red spot on most scales of the flank. However, fish that are suppressed by a dominant conspecific can be very pale, almost white. It is fascinating to observe a tank with Badis and try to understand the \"language of fishes\" spoken in changing coloration.</p>"
    }
  ],
